      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The smallest useful word — and the accent on it is doing real work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: sí and si",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sí = yes. One syllable, said see."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The written accent is not decoration — it is the whole difference between two words:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sí (with the accent) = yes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "si (no accent) = if."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "They sound almost identical, so Spanish uses the mark to tell them apart on the page: Sí, si quieres = \"Yes, if you want.\" Miss the accent and you've written \"if\" where you meant \"yes.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: where sí comes from",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sí is Latin sīc, \"thus, so\" — answering a question with \"just so,\" exactly the move Latin also made with ita (\"thus\" = yes, from the Latin yes/no lesson). Its Romance cousins kept the same root:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Latin sīc",
                ", which gives:"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "Latin sīc: Spanish. , which gives:: sí.",
                "Latin sīc: Italian. , which gives:: sì.",
                "Latin sīc: Portuguese. , which gives:: sim."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "French is the odd one out — it went a completely different way for \"yes\" (oui), which its own lesson tells. But say sí, sì, sim in a row and you hear one Latin word answering across three countries."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
