      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The easiest word yet — and it quietly does two jobs Spanish never splits apart."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: no",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "no = no. Said noh, a clean single vowel."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is Latin nōn with almost nothing worn off — just the final consonant gone. Compare the family, all from the same nōn:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Latin nōn",
                ", which gives:"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "Latin nōn: Spanish. , which gives:: no.",
                "Latin nōn: Italian. , which gives:: no.",
                "Latin nōn: French. , which gives:: non."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: one word, two jobs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Here's the thing English splits and Spanish doesn't. English has \"no\" (the answer) and \"not\" (inside a sentence) — two different words. Spanish uses no for both:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "as the answer: ¿Hablas inglés? — No."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "as the negator, placed right before the verb: No hablo inglés = \"I do not speak English.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So learning no gives you the whole of Spanish negation for free: to make any sentence negative, drop no in front of the verb. One little word, two everyday jobs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
